{# bennu.description: Log the value at debug level, labelled with the expression that computed it -#}
{# bennu.applies: any -#}
{#-
  A postfix template: typed after a value's dot — orders.logv — it replaces the value with what follows.
  The template's name is what you type after the dot.

  bennu.applies says which values it is offered on, comma-separated (any of them will do):
    any, reference, primitive, boolean, number, string, array, iterable, collection, map,
    optional, closeable, throwable — or a class name, com.acme.Order, for that class and its subtypes.
  bennu.level: 9 offers it only on a module at that Java level or later.

  The text is a snippet — $1, $2 and so on are the stops Tab walks through, ${1:name} is one with a
  default, and $0 is where the caret ends. Jinja runs first, with
    expr, type, type_simple, name, element_type, element_name, level, var, indent,
    file_name, class_name, package, date, year
-#}
log.debug("{{ expr }} = {}", {{ expr }});$0
